---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import NavBar from '../components/NavBar.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
---

<BaseLayout title="Install" description="Install amber on macOS or Linux, from source or Homebrew. C++17 compiler required.">
  <NavBar />
  <main>
    <h1>Install</h1>

    <section class="section">
      <p>
        Prefer binaries? <a href="/download">Get Amber</a> for prebuilt
        Homebrew, .deb, .rpm, or tarball, no compiler needed. This page covers
        building from source.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider-thick" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>macOS</h2>
      <h3>Homebrew (recommended)</h3>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>brew install {site.homebrewTap}/{site.homebrewFormula}</pre>
      </div>
      <p>
        Installs the prebuilt <code>amber</code> (TUI), <code>amber-cli</code>
        (headless) and <code>amber-bench</code> (benchmark harness) binaries
        with no compilation. Apple Silicon only.
      </p>
      <p>
        Or use the one-line installer (also installs Homebrew if you don't have
        it):
      </p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>/bin/bash -c "$(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/{site.homebrewTap}/main/install.sh)"</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Build from source</h2>
      <h3>Prerequisites</h3>
      <p>amber requires a C++17 compiler and a few development libraries:</p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre># macOS (Homebrew)
brew install curl ncurses pkg-config

# Debian / Ubuntu
sudo apt install build-essential libcurl4-openssl-dev libncurses-dev

# Arch Linux
sudo pacman -S base-devel curl ncurses

# Fedora / RHEL
sudo dnf install gcc-c++ libcurl-devel ncurses-devel</pre>
      </div>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li>A C++17 compiler (g++ or clang++)</li>
        <li>GNU make</li>
        <li>libcurl (development headers)</li>
        <li>ncurses (development headers, for the TUI client)</li>
        <li>OpenSSL (for HTTPS, pulled in by libcurl)</li>
        <li>nlohmann/json is vendored under include/ (no separate install needed)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        No Python or Node.js runtime is required. The test infrastructure has
        been deliberately moved away from Python dependencies.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h3>Build & Run</h3>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>git clone https://github.com/jtrefon/amber
cd amber
make

# Run the TUI
./amber

# Run headless
./amber-cli

# Run benchmarks (hermetic mode, no model needed)
./amber-bench run</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Configuration</h2>
      <p>amber reads from <code>amber.conf</code> in the project root and <code>~/.config/amber/</code> for global settings:</p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre># amber.conf (project-local, only data paths)
system_prompt=prompts/system.md
tools_prompt=prompts/tools.md

# Provider config lives in ~/.config/amber/providers/
# Example: ~/.config/amber/providers/llama.conf
api_base=http://localhost:8080/v1
model=Qwopus-27B-Fusion</pre>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Plugins</h2>
      <p>
        Amber ships with bundled plugins for providers (Anthropic, Gemini,
        OpenRouter, DeepSeek, KiloCode, OpenCode, CommandCode, Custom,
        OpenAI-compatible), core tools, and metrics. Plugins are controlled
        at runtime:
      </p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>/get plugin              # list plugins and their state
/set plugin anthropic on  # enable a provider plugin
/set plugin anthropic off # disable (unwinds cleanly)</pre>
      </div>
      <p>
        Plugin state is persisted in
        <code>~/.config/amber/plugins/&lt;id&gt;/plugin.conf</code>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>What Happens Next</h2>
      <p>When you run <code>./amber</code>, you'll see the welcome screen, the command drawer, and the slash command system:</p>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li><code>/set model</code>: switch models at runtime</li>
        <li><code>/get model list</code>: list available models</li>
        <li><code>/set provider</code>: switch provider configurations</li>
        <li><code>/get plugin</code>: list plugins and their state</li>
        <li><code>/set plugin &lt;id&gt; on|off</code>: enable or disable a plugin</li>
        <li><code>/compress</code>: force compression of conversation history</li>
        <li><code>/set policy</code>: configure approval rules</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <hr class="divider" />

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Build System</h2>
      <p>amber uses a standard <code>configure</code> + <code>make</code> build system. The <code>GNUmakefile</code> auto-runs <code>./configure</code> to generate <code>Makefile</code> from <code>Makefile.in</code>:</p>
      <div class="terminal">
        <pre>make          # build everything (lib + cli + tui)
make test     # run unit tests
make check    # smoketest + build hygiene
make lint     # run clang-tidy
make analyze  # run cppcheck
make clean    # remove .o / .d / binaries
make distclean # also removes the generated Makefile</pre>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
